{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And you shall remember HaShem, etc.</b> — meaning, one must set his mind upon His goodness, for it comes from HaShem, and this thing will constantly arouse him to acknowledge his Creator and His providence over him. Now, the very first strategy of a person's evil impulse is to make him forget this matter, and through this path it will enter to destroy him. And perhaps it is for this reason that Scripture juxtaposes after this <b>and it shall be if you forget you shall forget</b>: meaning, if you forget this matter — that HaShem is the one who does good to you, etc. — your end will be that you forget HaShem, and your end will be to go after other gods — and not in thought alone, but in deed, as it says ״וַעֲבַדְתָּם״ — and not a form of worship that is not defined by a prohibition, but ״וְהִשְׁתַּחֲוִיתָ לָהֶם״, which is a form of worship defined by a prohibition for every idolatry in the world, as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 60a): one who bows to Mercurius is liable even though its prescribed worship is not done thus — you thus learn that this remembrance is a great fence for the worship of HaShem, and from its absence the Satan will drag a person down from level to level below it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:16:02.067339659Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}